A||||13||Galen||Ideals of a young southern man pt. 2||||8||
Z||000000||Galen||05-10-2001||08:41 AM||||oKie, part two  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As I was watching MTV last night as I normally do whenever there is something good on. I was watching a special called "Fight For Your Rights: When Hate Goes Pop". I caught it and actually started watching it becuz they were talking about the character "Fat Bastard" from Austin Powers: The Spy Who Shagged Me. They were talking about how that was displaying 'hate' towards overweight people. How many people here believe that? or would you mak the same comment at the TV as I did, "Get Over It". There was one girl on there that said she first saw it on her birthday, and it made her feel really bad because of the character. It kinda makes me wonder how she ranks the movie, if she puts it in the same catagory as say, CNN, or CSPAN? <P>There was more to that show than just that, but do to personal restraint and wanting to keep some flaming away I will stay away from the rest of that show. But I must say, "They" say this country and world has become desensitized to hate, crime, and violence. But in my opinion, Its just the opposite.  This country and world have become supersensitive to everything. No matter what ANYONE does, someone will be offended by it. A movie, specially a comedic movie, poking fun at a fat guy(no I wont use overweight anymore). Do I feel offended by it? Not one bit, but then again I am a very skinny person. If someone was poking fun at skinny people would I be offended? I highly doubt it. In fact, I am sure I wouldn't be. If someone pokes fun at southern people, does that offend me? I am from NC, the south, that wouldn't offend me one bit. <P>In conclusion, Is it just me or would you tell most ppl to 'Get Over It' as well. Should I get over being so 'insensitive'? Am I really? Or one of the many who see that this country and world is full of sh!t.<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||05-10-2001||09:05 AM||||While I can understand the sentiments of people who have been picked on or teased, it seems that the most rapidly growing segment of American (and maybe other) society is the "Professional Victim". I try to treat people the same way that I'd like to be treated, but I'm really tired of hearing some of the whining.<BR>As you said, these people need to just get over it. Life is not fair. Never has been. Never will be. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||05-10-2001||09:14 AM||||No, you're totally right, Galen.  This country and this world are increasingly full of sh!t.  I read yesterday that a private school in New York is refusing to observe Mother's Day because children with two gay parents might be offended.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.jewishworldreview.com/cols/jonah.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jewishworldreview.com/cols/jonah.html</A> <P>Well, gee...we've observed Mother's Day for decades without worrying about the feelings of kids whose mothers died young or ran off with carnies.  But I guess the feelings of a child adopted by a gay couple must be extra special.<P>I find it disturbing that most people who preach tolerance are the most intolerant people around.  All you have to do to test this theory is express a different opinion than one of these folks and you'll quickly find out just how intolerant they really are.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||05-10-2001||09:40 AM||||Well, just to add the perspective of a "fat guy."  I wasn't offended by Austin Powers, or any of the characters in the movie, including Fat Bastard.  It was in fun and not malicious.  The problem comes in when people have a low self esteem and a stereotype or exaggerated characature makes them uncomfortable with something about themself.  <P>Yes, I believe the country, and world, is overly sensitive to being politically correct and worrying about offending someone.  It's everyone's second guessing themselves because they are so afraid of making offense that has everyone getting offended.<P>Here's something else to think about though.  If Fat Bastard had been a parody of black people instead of fat people, can you imagine the backlash that would have ensued?  A 7" tall black athlete carrying a basketball and fried chicken with him everywhere - hat on backwards, and only talking in bad rap references.  It would have been done in the same fashion as Fat Bastard, but the NAACP, Jesse Jackson, and any other black organization you can imagine would be suing Mike Meyers for everything he has.<P>Racism and prejudice will continue to exist as long as people are obsessed with whether or not they are being discriminated against.  It's a self-perpetuating cycle.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Cheever2||05-10-2001||09:58 AM||||Lots of good comments made here so far.  But the one about...<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I am a very skinny person. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>...I hate you.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> j/k   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
